Explain the anatomical structure and function of the mandible in the human body, including its role in the skeletal system and its relationship with other facial bones.
The mandible, also known as the lower jaw or jawbone, is the largest, strongest, and lowest bone in the human face. It forms the lower jaw and holds the lower teeth in place. The mandible articulates with the left and right temporal bones at the temporomandibular joints, allowing for movement of the jaw. The body of the mandible is curved, with two surfaces and two borders, and is marked in the midline by a faint ridge indicating the mandibular symphysis, the line of junction of the two pieces of which the bone is composed at an early stage.

The mandible plays a crucial role in the skeletal system, forming the lower jaw and providing a foundation for the lower teeth. It is the only movable bone of the skull, excluding the ossicles of the middle ear. The mandible is composed of the body, which gives structure to the chin, and the ramus, which connects the body to the temporal bone.

In terms of its relationship with other facial bones, the mandible sits beneath the maxilla and forms the lower jaw. It is connected to the temporal bones at the temporomandibular joints, allowing for movement of the jaw. The mandible also articulates with the hyoid bone and the cervical vertebrae, forming part of the human head.

