Explain the primary function of fish gills in relation to water
Fish gills are primarily responsible for exchanging gases, such as oxygen and carbon dioxide, with the water. They also facilitate the transfer of ions and water, as well as the exchange of acids and ammonia. Additionally, fish gills play a crucial role in osmoregulation, allowing fish to regulate the amount of water and salt in their bodies. In saltwater fish, gills help to remove excess salt, while in freshwater fish, they help to absorb water.
