What is regicide and what are its historical and cultural connotations?
Regicide is the deliberate killing of a monarch or the person responsible for the killing of a person of royalty. Historically, it has been associated with the judicial execution of a king after a trial, as seen in the case of Charles I of England. 

Regicide has cultural connotations that vary across different societies. In some ancient cultures, it was a ritualistic practice to kill the king at the end of their 7-year rule, symbolizing the end of an identity and the beginning of a new life chapter. In other contexts, it has been used as a metaphor for the public's treatment of celebrities, where a star's popularity fades and they are forgotten.

Regicide also has historical significance in relation to the concept of the divine right of kings, where monarchs were believed to have a divinely anointed authority to rule. An attack on a king by one of his own subjects was seen as a grave offense, and the term regicide has been used to describe such acts throughout history.
